## THE FINDING — SUPERSEDED, kept for the trajectory detail (see §5.121)
Against build 136, n=12, qythos9 does one of exactly two things with a repo URL:
  * **clones and answers perfectly — 4/12.** Nothing to fix.
  * **strips the URL to its BASENAME — 6/12, the dominant failure:**
        git ls-tree -r upstream.git --name-only | grep '^handlers/' | sort
          -> fatal: Not a valid object name upstream.git      (rc 128)
        [byte-identical repeat]
          -> fatal: Not a valid object name upstream.git
        [repeat guard nudges once, then stops the turn]

**The missing hypothesis is the same one ("the command itself cannot answer
this question") but the SURFACE IS THE ERROR PATH, NOT THE EMPTY PATH.** And
this is the sharper version: the model is not mishearing an ambiguous silence,
it is ignoring a specific, correct, complete error message — git names the
exact problem — and then re-issuing the identical command. No URL appears in
the failing command at all, which is why no URL detector could ever see it.

RESOLVED 2026-08-26: the repeat guard fires here and DOES its job (97% stop
repeating). Exposure is real archive-wide — 39% of repeat-nudges follow a
failing call, and the biggest contributor is not git but `replace_lines`
returning "NOT applied — would introduce a SyntaxError" byte-identically four
times. But the branch is outcome-neutral, so there is nothing to win. See the
top section.

## Premise correction (measured 2026-08-26, supersedes the 08-15 scoping)
The silent class is NOT "local-only git subcommands take URLs". Handed a URL
where a revision goes, git is loud: `show|log|rev-list|diff-tree` ->
`fatal: invalid object name 'file'`; `cat-file` -> `too many arguments`.
The silent class is exactly **a URL landing in a PATHSPEC position**:
`ls-tree`, `ls-files`, `log -- <url>`, `diff -- <url>`. (`git grep -- <url>`
is silent but exits 1, so the error-side guards already see it.)

## The case, and what three failed drafts cost (all recorded in check.py)
`evals/cases/git-url-empty` — needs `setup.sh` for three load-bearing reasons:
 1. The workspace must BE a git repo. Outside one the trap is a loud
    `fatal: not a git repository`, i.e. no trap.
 2. Upstream must be reachable ONLY as a URL. Draft 2 used `file://` and
    measured nothing — the URL spells out a path, so the model ls'd it, found
    `upstream.git`, cd'd in, queried it locally. Correct answer, zero URLs.
    Now a **`git daemon` on loopback:9419**, base `/tmp/locode-eval-gitd`,
    started on demand by setup.sh. Fully offline, no network, deterministic.
 3. Nothing is vendored in the workspace. Draft 1 put the copy at `handlers/`,
    so the pathspec matched the LOCAL tree and the trap printed a confident
    wrong answer. Draft 3 kept a stale copy at `vendor/handlers/` and the model
    answered by READING it. Every filename in the answer now exists only at the
    far end of the URL.
Leftover daemon after a sweep: `pkill -f 'git daemon.*9419'`.

